WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.810
Like anyone, the stars of the MCU mess up
sometimes, and the cast of Captain Marvel

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:05.480
was no exception.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.360
Despite seasoned actors and Oscar winners
filling the movie's roster, lines were forgotten,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.269
blocking was totally bungled, and goofiness
got the best of the stars more often than

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:14.820
you might think.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.440
Brie Larson carried the weight of Captain
Marvel on her shoulders, and spent months

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.880
preparing to play the high-flying heroine
as best as she possibly could.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.560
She devoted hours of her days to working out
in the gym, stuck to a strict diet, and even

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.060
visited the U.S. Air Force Academy for some
up-close-and-personal priming.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.240
But when Larson acquired all that newfound
strength, knowledge, and know-how, she apparently

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.760
lost an important basic skill.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.440
Multiple times during filming, Larson got
mixed up on the direction she was supposed

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.520
to go and what she was meant to do with her
body.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.070
"Where's my other hand supposed to go?

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:46.920
I don't know!"

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.480
Larson again gets directionally confused while
filming a fight scene with Talos actor Ben

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.420
Mendelsohn, who also could have used a helping
hand in the blocking department.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.519
The two rehearsed their tussle one way, but
when they got in front of the cameras, everything

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:00.519
was backwards.

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:01.519
"Other side!

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:02.519
Get on the other side!

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:06.850
I know we rehearsed it this way, but it's
this way now!"

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.660
Because the best things come in threes, Larson
tops these shenanigans off by barreling down

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.900
a hallway…before realizing that she's running
in the wrong direction.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:18.260
"I forgot I gotta go the other way!"

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.480
Rarely do actors nail a scene in one take.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.040
Their performance may be spot-on, but the
lighting might be totally wonky.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:29.410
Maybe the set design is impeccable and the
mood exactly right, but the line delivery

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:30.000
isn't seamless.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.680
Like we said before: no one in the movie industry
is immune to mistakes.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.570
For the Captain Marvel crew, though, one scene
didn't have just a single hiccup, it went

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:39.360
wrong in almost every way imaginable.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.880
The sequence in question is the one in which
Brie Larson's Carol Danvers, Ben Mendelsohn's

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.900
Talos, Samuel L. Jackson's Nick Fury, and
Lashana Lynch's Maria Rambeau escape from

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:53.050
the Kree warriors and the Starforce squad
in pursuit of Mar-Vell's hidden laboratory

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.050
that served as a refuge center for displaced
Skrulls.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:59.700
On-screen, the scene was flawless, but one
take turned hilariously disastrous.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.420
We're talking rogue kitten, lighting mishap,
whole-cast-breaking-character disastrous.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.140
Look at this unbridled mess:

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.240
"This is the worst take of anything that's
ever happened."

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:16.720
Worst?

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:17.170
Maybe.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:17.900
Most hilarious?

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:18.600
Oh, you bet!

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.360
If you thought the blooper featuring a real
cat playing Goose was rib-tickling, there

00:02:23.361 --> 00:02:27.569
are plenty of silly outtakes that find the
cast interacting with a fake feline, too.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:31.819
One take shows Samuel L. Jackson looking half-sad,
half-bemused to find out he's shooting with

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:35.260
a stand-in cat, especially since he keeps
getting fur on his nose.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.420
In another, Lashana Lynch can't keep it together
while Jackson holds a faux Goose and keeps

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.860
talking to it as if it was the real deal.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:42.320
"Good kitty!"

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.780
That's not all the feline fun the actors had
on set.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.760
In a third outtake, Brie Larson's Carol throws
a prop cat at Ben Mendelsohn's Talos, who

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:57.280
is deathly afraid of Goose since the cuddly
creature is actually a shapeshifting, pocket-dimension-holding

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.989
alien known as a Flerken.

00:02:58.989 --> 00:03:01.400
When she tosses the kitty at Talos, well…see
for yourselves:

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.020
"That's a no!"

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.659
Remember that scene in the film where Phil
Coulson and Nick Fury team up to find out

00:03:08.659 --> 00:03:11.519
what the heck is going on after Carol Danvers
crash-lands on Earth?

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.080
Y'know, after she destroys a Blockbuster,
and brings a pack of Skrulls trailing behind

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:15.620
her?

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.680
You'll recall that the dynamic duo was attacked
by Skrulls, and it's soon revealed that the

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.840
Coulson sitting in the passenger seat of Fury's
car isn't the S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.400
rookie at all.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.260
He's really a Skrull who shapeshifted into
Coulson.

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:29.749
The two fight for control of the steering
wheel, with Fury ultimately winning when he

00:03:29.749 --> 00:03:31.740
deliberately crashes the car and kills the
Skrull.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.040
That scene might've been wild to watch, but
the process of filming must've given the cast

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:40.340
and crew a heaping helping of headaches, if
this version of the same scene is any indication,

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:40.900
of course:

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.080
"Hold on!"

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:46.480
"S---."

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:47.620
"It's okay."

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:48.780
"No, it's not!"

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.279
We aren't privy to their medical histories,
but judging by the Captain Marvel gag reel,

00:03:53.279 --> 00:03:58.200
it looks like both Brie Larson and Ben Mendelsohn
might have a serious case of sweaty palms.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.030
Larson and Mendelsohn continuously dropped
things during shooting, ruining takes, but

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.319
showing that, beneath all those flashy prosthetics
and CGI effects, they're just as human as

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:06.860
their fans.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.760
The outtake reel finds clumsiness getting
the best of Larson while filming the scene

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.079
in which Carol Danvers retrieves the Tesseract from Mar-Vell's laboratory

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:14.780
orbiting Earth.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.260
"Aww, you broke it!"

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:22.020
Slippery fingers plagued Mendelsohn when he
was trying to perform a cool trick with Talos'

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:22.560
scepter.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.580
He can be seen tossing it up in the air ever-so-slightly,
but having trouble grabbing hold of the darn

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.840
thing when it comes back down.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.740
Each time he attempts the move, Mendelsohn
fumbles and the staff falls to the ground.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.060
After bleating repeatedly...

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.960
...and dropping his props over and over again
on the Captain Marvel set, Ben Mendelsohn

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.509
brought his silly streak to an end with a
rousing rendition of the 1960s tune "Release

00:04:47.509 --> 00:04:51.419
Me," apparently because he was worn-out from
filming and just wanted to wrap for the day,

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:55.810
peel off his facial prosthetics, scrub away
all his green Skrull makeup, and relax.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.220
After he ends a take, Mendelsohn declares
in a dramatic tone…

00:04:58.640 --> 00:04:59.900
"Thank you very much, goodnight!"

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:04.700
When no one answers back, he starts singing
the opening lines of the 1966 track popularized

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.160
by Engelbert Humperdinck, because…why not?

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.060
"Please release me!"

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:10.920
"No."

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.600
"Let me go!"

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:13.600
"Nope.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:14.840
Nope.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:15.850
Not happening!"

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:20.090
Despite that "Not happening," this round of
shooting inevitably ended with Mendelsohn's

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.480
wish finally being granted, and he was, indeed,
released.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.840
We're just happy that on this particular day,
he let his golden voice be heard.

